62	 Do Not Build Another Passport Platform — The Second Engine The Second Engine Contents Method ☾ Dark Essay 5 of 7 · Digital product passport / battery-passport integration Do Not Build Another Passport Platform On 18 February 2027, a second-life battery integrator placing a repurposed industrial battery over 2 kWh on the EU market does not merely inherit the donor battery's record.
48	The introduction asks about one production event: “Which finished battery will you place after 18 February, who is the economic operator, and can you trace ten planned units to donor passports, tests and BMS records?” A company without a dated batch is a research interview, not a prospect.
<p><strong>Customer twenty</strong> is a managed exception operation. Supplier reminders, validation, lineage checks, identifier registration, change approval and publication are automated. Humans resolve missing donor links, conflicting measurements, status changes and questions whose legal treatment has moved. The recurring value comes from real state changes, not an invented annual report. Acquisition repeats through battery compliance firms, test laboratories, recycling networks and DPP implementation partners, with one rule: no partner may both own the customer and silently substitute the company at renewal.</p>
<p>The accumulating asset is a regression-tested mapping library: legal data point to canonical field to source evidence to provider payload; fixtures for registry/provider conformance; supplier evidence templates; provenance and access-control patterns; and measured failure modes from real onboarding. It is not a database of customer battery secrets. Contracts should leave raw and transformed customer data with the customer, guarantee full export, restrict cross-customer reuse, and permit reuse only of generic mappings, code, empty templates and de-identified operational metrics.</p>
<p>Verification must be explicit. The system can validate presence, type, range, signature, lineage and approval. It cannot prove that a supplier's composition declaration is true or that a laboratory test was competent. Each field records source, issuer, date, transformation and approval; derived values preserve their inputs and algorithm version. Public, authority-only and legitimate-interest views are generated from one controlled record, not copied into three spreadsheets. Where access rules remain pending, the implementation uses a reversible policy table and a test credential—not a hard-coded interpretation.</p>
</section>

<section class="competition">
<h2>The incumbent gets a turn</h2>
<p>There is no platform gap. Siemens offers standardized machine-to-machine exchange and passport management through a REST API. <!-- CHECK: 05-siemens-api --> Minespider markets battery-passport tooling with advanced API connectivity. <!-- CHECK: 05-minespider --> Spherity markets templates, APIs, configurable access and a white-label-ready product. <!-- CHECK: 05-spherity-product --> Spherity specifically says consultancies can put VERA under their own brand for clients. <!-- CHECK: 05-spherity-channel --> These are vendor descriptions, not legal conclusions. The responsible economic operator still carries the passport duty. <!-- CHECK: 05-operator-responsibility --></p>
<p><strong>Circularise is the direct test, not a passive host.</strong> Its own battery-passport page describes an ongoing evidence operation rather than a final QR screen. <!-- CHECK: 05-circularise --></p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Circularise's marketed workflow</th><th>What remains for this offer</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Supplier evidence</td><td>Requests, normalises and reviews declarations, certificates and field evidence.</td><td>No feature edge. Only accountable human pursuit of missing or rejected evidence across the customer's chosen host.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Field ownership</td><td>Maps passport fields to owners, suppliers and systems, then automates evidence requests.</td><td>No edge unless the buyer pays for a managed exception SLA instead of operating Circularise itself.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Traceability</td><td>Links identifiers, batch history, supplier evidence and claims into an auditable trail.</td><td>Test multi-donor second-life lineage and the new battery record. Do not claim an edge until Circularise fails that authorised test.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Access and lifecycle</td><td>Publishes role-governed QR/API views and maintains repair, reuse and state-of-health updates.</td><td>Only exception ownership remains defensible: a named operator resolves conflicts and documents approvals.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Systems</td><td>Plans maintained-record integrations with ERP, PLM, LCA and compliance systems.</td><td>Provider neutrality may matter: one canonical export and test suite can target several passport hosts.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Neutrality, second-life lineage and managed exceptions are hypotheses, not established moats. Circularise may satisfy all three through its native workflow or services. A customer's ERP team may also generate the payload at source. <!-- CHECK: 05-erp-substitute --> Formal interoperability increases that pressure. The Regulation requires transferable data without vendor lock-in. <!-- CHECK: 05-operator-responsibility --> The registry indexes rather than stores the complete record. <!-- CHECK: 05-registry-live --> Those rules protect exit but commoditise translation.</p>
<p>Use an incumbent to reach production, while keeping customer-controlled export and a provider-neutral test suite. Kill this offer if two qualified buyers accept Circularise's native workflow, without paid external exception management, after testing their real second-life data. In that case, an entrant would duplicate a product rather than own a neglected operation.</p>
</section>

<div class="counter">
<h2>The capable failure</h2>
<p>The company chooses the right niche, partners with a competent battery lawyer, ships ten valid test passports, builds clean connectors and wins six second-life integrators. Then the standards and access act settle well. Donor OEMs expose reliable passport links; test-equipment vendors export the canonical fields; the leading DPP providers add second-life templates, lineage and registry submission; customers' ERP integrators map the remaining data during ordinary implementation.</p>
<p>The founders have done nothing wrong. Their interoperability work helped make the problem routine. But each customer places relatively few high-value stationary systems, lifecycle updates are rare, and the supposedly recurring evidence queue shrinks after onboarding. Procurement compares a €42,000 annual managed retainer with a platform module and twenty days of internal quality-engineer time. The platform vendor absorbs the connector into its licence; the battery adviser keeps the occasional interpretation. This company is squeezed out of the middle by the standardisation it correctly anticipated. It can survive as project integration, but not reach the recurring mature-year model.</p>
</div>

<section class="monday">
<h2>The first ninety days</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Days 1–12:</strong> ask TÜV SÜD and one fallback specialist for a capped €4,000 interpretation and testing-boundary review. Cover the product category, placing operator, Articles 45 and 77–78, and open implementation questions. Require one partner-supported prospect introduction.</li>
<li><strong>Days 1–25:</strong> interview ten in-scope prospect types. Require a dated 2027 production family, estimated unit count, donor-source map, current system of record and named budget owner. Record whether the buyer already has a DPP platform or ERP programme.</li>
<li><strong>Days 15–35:</strong> test Circularise and one fallback against real workflow fields: evidence requests, ownership, lineage, roles, lifecycle updates, ERP/PLM integration, export and exceptions. Use the Commission registry testing environment; do not build a production host.</li>
<li><strong>Days 25–55:</strong> build a canonical evidence record and ten synthetic or customer-authorised test passports. Exercise one original-to-new lineage, one BMS/test import, public/restricted views and a full export. Mark pending legal rules in configuration.</li>
<li><strong>Days 35–75:</strong> offer at least five €12,000 readiness sprints, 50% upfront, with the production prices stated before sale. A free workshop does not count.</li>
<li><strong>Days 60–90:</strong> deliver one paid sprint and ask for the €30,000 onboarding plus €3,500 monthly managed-data order. Cap all pre-evidence cash at <strong>€25,000</strong>, including legal advice, provider sandbox, security review, travel and translation.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Pass:</strong> one operator arrives through a named partner, pays for the sprint and supplies authorised donor, BMS and test evidence. It completes ten linked records and signs a production order or dated conditional order at the stated price. The map must show recurring change beyond issuance. <strong>Kill:</strong> the product is outside scope, five offers yield no paid sprint, or donor data cannot be obtained. Kill also if no recurring work supports the retainer. Kill if two qualified buyers accept Circularise's native workflow without paid external exception management. A standards delay calls for reversible mappings, not an invented obligation.</p>
</section>

<li><strong>Days 25–55:</strong> build a canonical evidence record and ten synthetic or customer-authorised test passports. Exercise one original-to-new lineage, one BMS/test import, public/restricted views and a full export. Mark pending legal rules in configuration.</li>
<li><strong>Days 35–75:</strong> offer at least five €12,000 readiness sprints, 50% upfront, with the production prices stated before sale. A free workshop does not count.</li>
<li><strong>Days 60–90:</strong> deliver one paid sprint and ask for the €30,000 onboarding plus €3,500 monthly managed-data order. Cap all pre-evidence cash at <strong>€25,000</strong>, including legal advice, provider sandbox, security review, travel and translation.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Pass:</strong> one operator arrives through a named partner, pays for the sprint and supplies authorised donor, BMS and test evidence. It completes ten linked records and signs a production order or dated conditional order at the stated price. The map must show recurring change beyond issuance. <strong>Kill:</strong> the product is outside scope, five offers yield no paid sprint, or donor data cannot be obtained. Kill also if no recurring work supports the retainer. Kill if two qualified buyers accept Circularise's native workflow without paid external exception management. A standards delay calls for reversible mappings, not an invented obligation.</p>
</section>

<div class="verdict-box">
<h2>Verdict</h2>
<p class="verdict">TEST THROUGH A PARTNER</p>
<p>The date and second-life responsibility justify a paid test now, but only beside a battery-regulation specialist and on top of an existing DPP provider. The single assumption that would most change the verdict is that small second-life operators experience enough continuing evidence and lifecycle work to pay roughly €42,000 a year after initial integration. If the readiness sprint finds a one-time mapping followed by rare updates, this is consultancy, not a scalable second engine.</p>
</div>

<div class="reading">
<h2>Reading</h2>
