 <main class="wrap">
 <p class="kicker">Essay 5 of 7 · Digital product passport / battery-passport integration</p>
 <h1>Do Not Build Another Passport Platform</h1>
-<p class="lede">On 18 February 2027, a second-life battery integrator placing a repurposed industrial battery over 2 kWh on the EU market does not merely inherit the donor battery's record. It takes responsibility for accurate passport information and must create a new passport linked to the original. <!-- CHECK: 05-second-life-transfer --> The opportunity is not a generic “DPP platform”: Siemens, Circularise, Minespider and Spherity are already there. It is the dirty operational layer between donor records, BMS tests, product data, access rules and one compliant publication route. That layer may earn recurring money—or disappear as soon as the platforms and source systems learn to speak.</p>
+<p class="lede">On 18 February 2027, a second-life battery integrator placing a repurposed industrial battery over 2 kWh on the EU market does not merely inherit the donor battery's record. It takes responsibility for accurate passport information and must create a new passport linked to the original. <!-- CHECK: 05-second-life-transfer --> The opportunity is not another platform. Siemens offers a battery-passport API. <!-- CHECK: 05-siemens-api --> Circularise markets battery-passport software. <!-- CHECK: 05-circularise --> Minespider markets battery-passport tooling. <!-- CHECK: 05-minespider --> Spherity markets a DPP product. <!-- CHECK: 05-spherity-product --> The opening is the operational layer between donor records, tests, access rules and a chosen publication route. It may earn recurring money—or disappear when those products and source systems learn to speak.</p>
 
 <section class="proposition">
 <h2>The proposition</h2>
 <p>The first customer is a Benelux company that assembles stationary energy-storage systems from batteries previously used in vehicles or other applications and expects to place its finished industrial batteries on the EU market after 18 February 2027. It is not a cell manufacturer or a vehicle OEM. It buys or receives donor packs or modules, tests and grades them, changes their purpose or configuration, integrates a BMS and enclosure, and becomes the economic operator for the second-life battery.</p>
-<p>The buyer is its COO, head of compliance or product-data lead. The purchase event is a dated production batch that crosses the 2027 application date and cannot ship with a spreadsheet standing in for a battery passport. The deliverable is a managed data operation: determine the applicable passport fields with qualified counsel; map each field to a source and responsible person; ingest donor passport references, bill-of-material and test/BMS data; preserve provenance and approvals; generate the QR/identifier handoff; submit required registration metadata; and publish through an existing passport provider chosen with the customer. After launch, the service monitors missing, changed and expiring evidence and handles new units and lifecycle updates.</p>
+<p>The buyer is its COO, head of compliance or product-data lead. The purchase event is a dated production batch that crosses the 2027 application date and cannot ship with a spreadsheet standing in for a battery passport. The deliverable is a managed data operation. It determines fields with qualified counsel, assigns sources and owners, ingests donor and test data, preserves approvals, prepares identifiers, submits registration metadata and publishes through the customer's chosen provider. After launch, the service monitors missing, changed and expiring evidence and handles new units and lifecycle updates.</p>
 <p>The business does not host the only copy of the passport, issue product conformity, calculate or certify carbon footprints, verify responsible sourcing, provide battery-safety engineering, or pretend to be the customer's legal adviser. It does not invent a blockchain or proprietary identifier. A battery lawyer interprets scope; testing and conformity bodies assess what requires them; a DPP provider stores and serves the record. The company owns the connectors, validation rules, evidence workflow and operating playbook.</p>
 <p>The substitute is a compliance consultant plus spreadsheets and manual uploads, the customer's ERP/PLM team, or the integration/professional-services arm of the selected DPP platform. That last substitute is the serious one. The operation wins only where source data remain too fragmented and lifecycle changes too frequent for “configure once, issue passports” to be true.</p>
 </section>
 
 <section class="changed">
 <h2>What changed</h2>
-<p><strong>The dated battery obligation is enacted.</strong> Article 77 of Regulation (EU) 2023/1542 says that from 18 February 2027 each light-means-of-transport battery, each industrial battery above 2 kWh and each electric-vehicle battery placed on the market or put into service must have an electronic battery passport. It includes model information and information specific to the individual battery. <!-- CHECK: 05-art77-scope --> Article 13 separately requires a QR code from the same date; for those covered categories it leads to the passport, and the marking must be visible, legible and indelible on the battery or, where that is not possible or warranted, on packaging and accompanying documents. Article 77(3) says the QR code links to a unique identifier assigned by the placing operator and requires both to comply with the ISO/IEC 15459 family listed there, or equivalent standards. <!-- CHECK: 05-qr-rule --></p>
+<p><strong>The dated battery obligation is enacted.</strong> Article 77 of Regulation (EU) 2023/1542 applies from 18 February 2027. Covered batteries are each light-means-of-transport battery, each industrial battery above 2 kWh and each electric-vehicle battery placed on the market or put into service. Each needs an electronic passport containing model and individual-battery information. <!-- CHECK: 05-art77-scope --> Article 13 requires a QR code from the same date. It leads to the passport for those categories. The marking must be visible, legible and indelible on the battery or, where appropriate, on packaging and accompanying documents. Article 77(3) links the code to a placing-operator-assigned identifier. Both must comply with the listed ISO/IEC 15459 family or equivalent standards. <!-- CHECK: 05-qr-rule --></p>
 <p>The narrow second-life chain has an additional trigger. Article 77(7) transfers responsibility for passport accuracy, completeness and currency to the economic operator that places a reused, repurposed or remanufactured battery on the market or puts it into service. It requires a new passport linked to the original passport or passports. Article 13(9) also requires new labels or markings describing the status change. <!-- CHECK: 05-second-life-transfer --> This is why the opening is better than generic passport advice: the operator must join lineage from potentially several donor records to a changed physical product.</p>
 <p>Responsibility cannot be outsourced away. Article 77(4) permits written authorisation for another operator to act, but leaves the placing operator responsible for accurate, complete and current information. The same article requires open standards, an interoperable, machine-readable, structured and searchable format, transfer through an open network and no vendor lock-in. <!-- CHECK: 05-operator-responsibility --> Article 78 requires authentication, integrity, role-based access, continued availability after the responsible operator ceases activity, and forbids an authorised processor from reusing passport data beyond the service unless specifically agreed. <!-- CHECK: 05-art78-controls --> The contract must therefore distinguish doing the work from owning the legal answer.</p>
