 <section class="competition">
 <h2>The incumbent gets a turn</h2>
-<p>There is no platform gap. Siemens offers a Battery Passport REST API with user and passport management, including create, update and retrieve operations. <!-- CHECK: 05-siemens-api --> Circularise markets model- and individual-battery data, QR access and permissions in one platform. <!-- CHECK: 05-circularise --> Minespider markets EU battery-passport tooling and an open API. <!-- CHECK: 05-minespider --> Spherity markets templates, APIs, secure access and a fully white-label-ready product. <!-- CHECK: 05-spherity-product --> Their claims of compliance are vendor claims; only the responsible economic operator carries the legal duty. But their existence means a new generic passport host starts behind on product, standards work, security evidence and integrations.</p>
-<p>Spherity explicitly invites consultancies and integrators to resell or white-label its software. <!-- CHECK: 05-spherity-channel --> That is both route and threat. Use such a platform to reach production quickly, but keep the customer contract, canonical export and provider-neutral test suite. Siemens can bundle its passport into an industrial digital stack; Circularise and Minespider can add onboarding services; an ERP vendor can generate the payload at source. The proposed company keeps margin only if it repeatedly solves evidence and change operations those products cannot economically standardise.</p>
-<p>Interoperability is not a slogan here. The Regulation itself requires open, transferable data without vendor lock-in, and the EU registry indexes rather than stores the full record. <!-- CHECK: 05-operator-responsibility --><!-- CHECK: 05-registry-live --> A provider that blocks export is a compliance risk as well as a commercial one. Yet formal interoperability can commoditise the connector. The more successful the standards become, the less anyone should pay merely to translate JSON. Margin must migrate toward data provenance, supplier follow-up, exception handling and accountable operations.</p>
+<p>There is no platform gap. Siemens offers standardized machine-to-machine exchange and passport management through a REST API. <!-- CHECK: 05-siemens-api --> Minespider markets battery-passport tooling with advanced API connectivity. <!-- CHECK: 05-minespider --> Spherity markets templates, APIs, configurable access and a white-label-ready product. <!-- CHECK: 05-spherity-product --> Spherity specifically says consultancies can put VERA under their own brand for clients. <!-- CHECK: 05-spherity-channel --> These are vendor descriptions, not legal conclusions. The responsible economic operator still carries the passport duty. <!-- CHECK: 05-operator-responsibility --></p>
+<p><strong>Circularise is the direct test, not a passive host.</strong> Its own battery-passport page describes an ongoing evidence operation rather than a final QR screen. <!-- CHECK: 05-circularise --></p>
+<div class="table-wrap"><table>
+<thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Circularise's marketed workflow</th><th>What remains for this offer</th></tr></thead>
