-<p>The model clears the owner-income target and retains cash only because customers pay a substantial retainer and platform licences stay out of revenue. If the managed work settles into one annual CSV upload, the retainer will be competed away. At €12 per event alone, 4,000 events produce €48,000: less than one engineer. Conversely, if every passport needs two hours of manual source chasing, 4,000 events consume roughly four full-time working years before review and support. The acceptable process must make the typical event mostly automatic and reserve human time for exceptions.</p>
-<p>Working capital deserves restraint. Bill sprints 50% upfront, onboarding 40/40/20 against evidence-map, test and production milestones, and retainers quarterly in advance. Keep provider licences on the customer's contract and do not finance laboratory work. The first 90 days need at most €25,000; a production company should preserve roughly €90,000 for six months of a small team's payroll and specialist commitments. That is almost the reader's full capital allowance, which is another reason not to build hosting infrastructure before payment.</p>
+<p>The model clears the owner-income target only because retainers carry the business and platform licences stay outside revenue. At €12 per event, 4,000 events produce €48,000: less than one engineer. Two manual hours per event would consume roughly four working years before support. The ordinary event must therefore be mostly automatic.</p>
+<p><strong>The workload bridge is a design hurdle, not observed performance.</strong> Assume 85% of 4,000 events pass straight through, with three minutes of monitoring and sampling each: 170 hours. The other 600 take 45 minutes: 450 hours. Four onboardings at 240 hours consume 960 hours. Twelve annual compliance reviews at 80 hours consume another 960. Support uses 600 hours; connector, rule and provider maintenance uses 800. Total planned delivery is 3,940 hours. Two engineers and one operations lead provide 4,800 delivery hours at 1,600 each, leaving 860 hours, or 18%, for incidents and overruns. Founder sales and rare escalations sit outside that capacity. If measured automation falls below 85%, do not add the twelfth customer.</p>
+<p><strong>Six months of mature obligations is €232,500.</strong> Labour plus specialist QA is €390,000 annually, so six months is €195,000. Including the €75,000 overhead allocation raises it by €37,500. The reader cannot cash-fund that reserve from €100,000.</p>
+<p>The hiring gate must finance the difference. Twelve quarterly retainers collected in advance provide €126,000. Hold €40,000 of prior retained profit, arrange a €40,000 committed receivables facility and preserve €26,500 of founder cash. Together those sources cover €232,500; onboarding deposits provide no assumed cushion. Founder cash remains inside the €100,000 limit, including the €25,000 test cap. If buyers refuse advance billing, retained profit is absent or the facility is unavailable, stay smaller rather than hire the mature team.</p>
 <p>Track economics by data object, not by customer logo: automated versus manual fields, evidence rejection rate, minutes per passport event, supplier follow-up days, schema-change rework, and exceptions requiring counsel. A customer with five product variants and one disciplined ERP may be cheaper than a one-product customer with ten donor sources. Price the data topology found in the sprint, not nominal passport count.</p>
 </section>
 
