 <section class="sale">
 <h2>The first sale</h2>
-<p>Build the first list from ten explicit prospect types: an EV-pack second-life integrator; bus-battery repurposer; forklift-battery remanufacturer; home-to-commercial storage upgrader; containerised BESS assembler using recovered modules; marine auxiliary-storage integrator; telecom backup-power remanufacturer; industrial UPS battery rebuilder; recycler launching a graded second-life product; and a battery leasing operator that becomes the placing economic operator after remanufacture. Screen out firms whose 2027 products are below 2 kWh, remain prototypes, or are placed by an OEM that retains all passport responsibility.</p>
-<p>The founder should approach through one named category of domain partner: a battery conformity/safety consultancy that will sign its own interpretation, not a generic sustainability agency. Add one passport-platform solutions partner so the diagnostic tests a real publication route. The introduction asks for a specific production event: “Which finished battery will you place on the market after 18 February, who is the economic operator, and can you trace ten planned units back to donor passports, tests and BMS records?” A company without a dated batch is a research interview, not a prospect.</p>
-<p>The first offer is a six-week <strong>€12,000 passport-readiness sprint</strong>, a proposed test price. It covers one finished product family and up to ten test units. Outputs are a counsel-reviewed applicability memo; a field-by-field responsibility and evidence matrix; lineage mapping to original passport identifiers; extraction of one authorised BMS/test dataset; role/access design marked “provisional” where the implementing act is pending; ten test-environment passport payloads; a provider comparison; and a fixed production quote. It excludes legal opinions beyond the scoped memo, conformity assessment and new source-data creation. Fifty per cent is paid before work.</p>
+<p>Build the first list from ten prospect types. Include EV-pack, bus-battery and forklift-battery repurposers; storage upgraders; recovered-module BESS assemblers; marine and telecom integrators; UPS rebuilders; recyclers; and post-remanufacture leasing operators. Screen out firms whose 2027 products are below 2 kWh, remain prototypes, or are placed by an OEM retaining passport responsibility.</p>
+<p><strong>TÜV SÜD is one candidate domain partner.</strong> It offers product-specific EU Battery Regulation interpretation, battery testing and documented technical-verification services. <!-- CHECK: 05-tuvsud-partner --> Buy a capped interpretation and testing-boundary review; require its specialist to introduce one qualified prospect. Do not give it exclusivity or the data-operation account. Its broad service range means it could absorb the work or own the relationship. <!-- CHECK: 05-tuvsud-partner --> Add one passport-provider contact so the diagnostic tests a real publication route.</p>
+<p>The introduction asks about one production event: “Which finished battery will you place after 18 February, who is the economic operator, and can you trace ten planned units to donor passports, tests and BMS records?”</p>
+<p>A company without a dated batch is a research interview, not a prospect.</p>
+<p>The first offer is a six-week <strong>€12,000 passport-readiness sprint</strong>, a proposed test price. It covers one product family and ten test units. Deliverables include a counsel-reviewed applicability memo, an evidence matrix, lineage mapping and extraction of one authorised BMS/test dataset. It also supplies provisional access design, ten test payloads, a provider comparison and a fixed production quote. It excludes broader legal opinions, conformity assessment and new source-data creation. Fifty per cent is paid before work.</p>
 <p>The COO or compliance lead signs. Operations, engineering, quality and IT each name one owner. The customer contracts directly with the passport host, so licence cost and continuity rights remain visible and there is no hidden reseller lock-in. English should work for cross-border suppliers and technical teams, but local production records, regulator contact and shop-floor evidence may be Dutch or German. At A2 Dutch the founder must budget professional translation and let the qualified local partner lead regulatory conversations.</p>
 <p>The required production price is €30,000 for integration/onboarding, then €3,500 per month for managed evidence and change operations plus €12 per newly issued or materially updated passport event. These are hurdles, not market facts. The platform licence, identifier issuance, lab work and legal opinions are billed directly to the customer. If a ten-unit sprint cannot justify that next-stage order, do not assume thousands of future passports will.</p>
 </section>
 
 <section class="economics">
 <h2>Does the money work?</h2>
-<p>A high-value stationary system may contain fewer passported units than a vehicle programme. Per-passport fees cannot carry this company. The retainer pays for the changing state: supplier evidence arrives, tests are rerun, a donor lineage is corrected, roles change, a unit is updated, a standard or data rule changes, or an authority asks for proof. The event fee disciplines volume; the operating retainer funds readiness.</p>
-<div class="table-wrap"><table>
+<p>Every figure in this section is a design hurdle, not a forecast or observed market performance. A high-value stationary system may contain fewer passported units than a vehicle programme. Per-passport fees cannot carry this company. The retainer pays for changing evidence, tests, lineage, roles, units and rules. The event fee disciplines volume; the operating retainer funds readiness.</p>
+<div class="ledger-wrap"><table class="ledger">
 <thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Revenue</th><th>Direct cost / gross margin</th><th>Operating result</th></tr></thead>
 <tbody>
 <tr><td><strong>Year 1:</strong> three readiness sprints; two onboardings; two managed customers live for an average six months; 200 issue/update events.</td><td>Sprints €36k; onboarding €60k; retainers €42k; events €2.4k. <strong>Total €140.4k.</strong></td><td>Battery-law and data-QA subcontractors €15k; test cloud/tooling €5k. <strong>Gross margin €120.4k (86%).</strong></td><td>Founder labour at €75k replacement cost plus €45k security, insurance, travel, translation and sales leaves approximately <strong>break-even</strong>. No owner distribution.</td></tr>
 <tr><td><strong>Mature test:</strong> twelve managed customers; four new onboardings; 4,000 issue/update events.</td><td>Retainers €504k; onboarding €120k; events €48k. <strong>Total €672k.</strong></td><td>Specialist data QA €60k; cloud/test systems €24k. <strong>Gross margin €588k (88%).</strong></td><td>Two integration engineers €160k; compliance-operations lead €70k; founder replacement salary €100k; legal, security, insurance, translation, travel and sales €75k. <strong>€183k remains</strong> before corporation tax and financing.</td></tr>
 </tbody></table></div>
-<p>The model clears the owner-income target and retains cash only because customers pay a substantial retainer and platform licences stay out of revenue. If the managed work settles into one annual CSV upload, the retainer will be competed away. At €12 per event alone, 4,000 events produce €48,000: less than one engineer. Conversely, if every passport needs two hours of manual source chasing, 4,000 events consume roughly four full-time working years before review and support. The acceptable process must make the typical event mostly automatic and reserve human time for exceptions.</p>
-<p>Working capital deserves restraint. Bill sprints 50% upfront, onboarding 40/40/20 against evidence-map, test and production milestones, and retainers quarterly in advance. Keep provider licences on the customer's contract and do not finance laboratory work. The first 90 days need at most €25,000; a production company should preserve roughly €90,000 for six months of a small team's payroll and specialist commitments. That is almost the reader's full capital allowance, which is another reason not to build hosting infrastructure before payment.</p>
+<p>The model clears the owner-income target only because retainers carry the business and platform licences stay outside revenue. At €12 per event, 4,000 events produce €48,000: less than one engineer. Two manual hours per event would consume roughly four working years before support. The ordinary event must therefore be mostly automatic.</p>
+<p><strong>The workload bridge is a design hurdle, not observed performance.</strong> Assume 85% of 4,000 events pass straight through, with three minutes of monitoring and sampling each: 170 hours. The other 600 take 45 minutes: 450 hours. Four onboardings at 240 hours consume 960 hours. Twelve annual compliance reviews at 80 hours consume another 960. Support uses 600 hours; connector, rule and provider maintenance uses 800. Total planned delivery is 3,940 hours. Two engineers and one operations lead provide 4,800 delivery hours at 1,600 each, leaving 860 hours, or 18%, for incidents and overruns. Founder sales and rare escalations sit outside that capacity. If measured automation falls below 85%, do not add the twelfth customer.</p>
+<p><strong>Six months of mature obligations is €232,500.</strong> Labour plus specialist QA is €390,000 annually, so six months is €195,000. Including the €75,000 overhead allocation raises it by €37,500. The reader cannot cash-fund that reserve from €100,000.</p>
+<p>The hiring gate must finance the difference. Twelve quarterly retainers collected in advance provide €126,000. Hold €40,000 of prior retained profit, arrange a €40,000 committed receivables facility and preserve €26,500 of founder cash. Together those sources cover €232,500; onboarding deposits provide no assumed cushion. Founder cash remains inside the €100,000 limit, including the €25,000 test cap. If buyers refuse advance billing, retained profit is absent or the facility is unavailable, stay smaller rather than hire the mature team.</p>
 <p>Track economics by data object, not by customer logo: automated versus manual fields, evidence rejection rate, minutes per passport event, supplier follow-up days, schema-change rework, and exceptions requiring counsel. A customer with five product variants and one disciplined ERP may be cheaper than a one-product customer with ten donor sources. Price the data topology found in the sprint, not nominal passport count.</p>
 </section>
 
